00:04:53.190 --> 00:04:54.780
in the enterprise.

00:04:54.780 --> 00:04:57.330
And so while we're
focused on innovation,

00:04:57.330 --> 00:05:01.290
we take a thoughtful approach
that not only is just

00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:04.350
about innovating in
tech but innovating

00:05:04.350 --> 00:05:06.360
in a way that actually
makes us better,

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:08.050
makes us perform better.

00:05:08.050 --> 00:05:11.760
So take David Sax's quote from,
"The Revenge of the Analog."

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:15.000
He says, "The process of
technological innovation

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:18.600
isn't a story of slow march
from good to better to best.

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:22.290
It's a series of trials that
helps us understand who we are

00:05:22.290 --> 00:05:25.092
and how we operate."

00:05:25.092 --> 00:05:27.050
So when it comes to
meetings and collaboration,

00:05:27.050 --> 00:05:30.920
our team is focused on making
things so much more better.

00:05:30.920 --> 00:05:33.770
But better in the
context of how we operate

00:05:33.770 --> 00:05:34.730
and how we work today.

00:05:37.054 --> 00:05:38.470
A lot of times in
meetings, you're

00:05:38.470 --> 00:05:41.320
trying to communicate
information.

00:05:41.320 --> 00:05:43.414
And yes, while we've
enabled video conferencing,

00:05:43.414 --> 00:05:44.830
sometimes it's
really hard for you

00:05:44.830 --> 00:05:48.430
to communicate and
express your idea freely.

00:05:48.430 --> 00:05:51.580
And it's very hard
to brainstorm,

00:05:51.580 --> 00:05:54.980
especially when you want
to walk to the whiteboard.

00:05:54.980 --> 00:05:56.690
So this is pretty essential.

00:05:56.690 --> 00:05:59.690
A lot of times we
communicate, as humans,

00:05:59.690 --> 00:06:01.970
in a very, very expressive way.

00:06:01.970 --> 00:06:03.890
And that sometimes gets
lost in translation

00:06:03.890 --> 00:06:07.760
when you're on the phone
or on a video call.

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:10.040
So take, for example,
the whiteboard.

00:06:10.040 --> 00:06:13.520
This is so basic,
and yet so powerful.

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:16.190
A lot of times when you have to
make important decisions or you

00:06:16.190 --> 00:06:17.689
need to brainstorm
with someone, you

00:06:17.689 --> 00:06:19.010
have to be present in the room.

00:06:19.010 --> 00:06:19.940
It's a blank canvas.

00:06:19.940 --> 00:06:21.870
And what's great about
a canvas is it's open

00:06:21.870 --> 00:06:23.870
and you can fill it with
anything that you want.

00:06:27.010 --> 00:06:29.980
So, as we talk
about the benefits

00:06:29.980 --> 00:06:32.050
of being able to
communicate and express

00:06:32.050 --> 00:06:34.630
with each other in meetings,
sometimes technology

00:06:34.630 --> 00:06:35.145
is limiting.

00:06:35.145 --> 00:06:37.270
And this is something that
we took to heart when we

00:06:37.270 --> 00:06:38.800
were thinking about Jamboard.

00:06:43.060 --> 00:06:47.620
We looked at other
digital whiteboards.

00:06:47.620 --> 00:06:50.980
And we felt that they had
quite a few things that

00:06:50.980 --> 00:06:54.730
didn't really quite help
you get better, right?

00:06:54.730 --> 00:06:58.720
So for example, some of the
screens were not high fidelity.

00:06:58.720 --> 00:07:02.590
They felt like you were
writing, and you couldn't really

00:07:02.590 --> 00:07:04.660
see it too well, or
there was a visible lag.

00:07:04.660 --> 00:07:08.110
Sometimes when you write, and
as you're drawing and writing,

00:07:08.110 --> 00:07:11.920
you started to see that
the ink print, like a half

00:07:11.920 --> 00:07:14.680
a centimeter away or
half an inch away.

00:07:14.680 --> 00:07:17.350
And sometimes that
really bothered people

00:07:17.350 --> 00:07:19.437
when they were trying to
just get their ideas out

00:07:19.437 --> 00:07:20.020
of their head.

00:07:20.020 --> 00:07:22.960
And so a lot of times, people
would stop using those tools

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:25.000
and just pick up a
whiteboard marker

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:26.800
and use the whiteboard
because it's faster.

00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:29.890
Your brain can think faster,
and you want your ideas

00:07:29.890 --> 00:07:31.710
to flow with it.

00:07:31.710 --> 00:07:33.990
In that process we
didn't necessarily--

00:07:33.990 --> 00:07:36.690
when we started looking
at designing Jamboard--

00:07:36.690 --> 00:07:39.340
we didn't want to make
it look like a black box.

00:07:39.340 --> 00:07:41.670
We wanted it to
inspire creativity.

00:07:41.670 --> 00:07:43.950
We wanted it to
exude collaboration.

00:07:43.950 --> 00:07:47.100
And so we'd spent a lot of
time in designing the Jamboard

00:07:47.100 --> 00:07:49.740
in a way that it felt
more like furniture

00:07:49.740 --> 00:07:56.620
in your workplace, and more fun,
rather than like a television.

00:07:56.620 --> 00:08:00.400
The last thing we noticed is
that buying this technology

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:03.040
often was very expensive,
and so we really

00:08:03.040 --> 00:08:06.070
spent a lot of time working with
our partners and our vendors,

00:08:06.070 --> 00:08:08.800
ensuring that we could come
up with a world class product

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:10.480
but yet, at an affordable price.

00:08:13.030 --> 00:08:16.530
So what's ideal is, we
wanted our digital whiteboard

00:08:16.530 --> 00:08:19.800
to be intuitive and simple,
just like a whiteboard.

00:08:19.800 --> 00:08:21.300
There's something
amazing about just

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:23.460
being able to walk up
to it, pick up a pen,

00:08:23.460 --> 00:08:25.240
and start writing.

00:08:25.240 --> 00:08:30.020
You'd never want home screens
and all kinds of applications

00:08:30.020 --> 00:08:31.880
in your face to decide
what you want to do.

00:08:31.880 --> 00:08:37.750
You just want to walk straight
up and get your ideas done.

00:08:37.750 --> 00:08:39.610
So that's why we made Jamboard.

00:08:39.610 --> 00:08:43.530
It's a whiteboard we imagined
for collaboration in the Cloud.

00:08:43.530 --> 00:08:46.030
This is a product that we're
really proud of here at Google.

00:08:46.030 --> 00:08:48.040
We've designed this
from the ground up.

00:08:48.040 --> 00:08:52.540
Everything from the silicon,
all the way out to the platform,

00:08:52.540 --> 00:08:53.710
to the app.

00:08:53.710 --> 00:08:57.258
And it's built on collaboration
at its foundation.

00:09:00.190 --> 00:09:05.240
We spent a lot of time on
the design, like I mentioned.

00:09:05.240 --> 00:09:09.060
One of our key principles
was to keep it simple.

00:09:09.060 --> 00:09:11.970
If you could draw or do
something on a whiteboard

00:09:11.970 --> 00:09:14.490
faster than you could
on a digital screen,

00:09:14.490 --> 00:09:16.044
we said we'd failed.

00:09:16.044 --> 00:09:17.460
And you'll notice
here in the demo

00:09:17.460 --> 00:09:20.670
just how quick it is
for you to get started.

00:09:20.670 --> 00:09:22.290
Drawing is always on.

00:09:22.290 --> 00:09:24.422
You can walk up to it
and just start drawing.

00:09:24.422 --> 00:09:26.380
And what's amazing is
the technology that we've

00:09:26.380 --> 00:09:28.860
put in here allows
for the things

00:09:28.860 --> 00:09:32.190
that you're naturally inclined
to do with a whiteboard.

00:09:32.190 --> 00:09:34.560
Where you can write with a
stylus, erase with a finger.

00:09:39.800 --> 00:09:43.160
Now, once we got the
basics done, of course,

00:09:43.160 --> 00:09:44.350
we can't stop there.

00:09:44.350 --> 00:09:45.500
We're Google.

00:09:45.500 --> 00:09:49.760
So we focused on how we can
make your ideas come to life.

00:09:49.760 --> 00:09:52.310
With intelligent features
like handwriting recognition

00:09:52.310 --> 00:09:56.420
and shape recognition to help
you express a lot of the ideas

00:09:56.420 --> 00:10:00.350
that you put together,
in a much faster way.

00:10:00.350 --> 00:10:03.230
Being able to go to the web
and capture content from there,

00:10:03.230 --> 00:10:04.670
or grab images.

00:10:04.670 --> 00:10:08.090
And of course, you know
sticky notes and stencils.

00:10:08.090 --> 00:10:10.820
And then lastly,
we also enabled--

00:10:10.820 --> 00:10:13.430
as JR mentioned, we started
actually on mobile devices.

00:10:13.430 --> 00:10:16.730
But we've enabled it for tablet
devices and for smartphones,

00:10:16.730 --> 00:10:18.185
both for Android and iOS.

00:10:22.280 --> 00:10:24.260
And of course, the
meeting doesn't just

00:10:24.260 --> 00:10:25.670
end with a whiteboard.

00:10:25.670 --> 00:10:28.340
That would be sad, if
all we did was just let

00:10:28.340 --> 00:10:31.520
you collaborate and end there.

00:10:31.520 --> 00:10:34.340
We allowed you to save your--

00:10:34.340 --> 00:10:36.099
all of your Jams
are saved in Drive.

00:10:36.099 --> 00:10:37.640
And not only are
they saved in Drive,

00:10:37.640 --> 00:10:40.700
but you can bring in
content from Google Drive

00:10:40.700 --> 00:10:41.990
into a Jamboard.

00:10:41.990 --> 00:10:45.110
We've also integrated with
Hangouts and Google Meet.

00:10:45.110 --> 00:10:48.740
So anytime you're in a
video conferencing call,

00:10:48.740 --> 00:10:51.151
you can actually present
your Jamboard to it.

