and you can see there's
a list of options

00:23:04.850 --> 00:23:07.340
and we have a few things
we can do from here.

00:23:07.340 --> 00:23:09.929
So the first one I wanted to
mention is sending a copy.

00:23:09.929 --> 00:23:11.470
There's a lot of
points in time where

00:23:11.470 --> 00:23:13.030
you might want to share
an artifact of what

00:23:13.030 --> 00:23:14.110
you've been working on.

00:23:14.110 --> 00:23:15.910
This is that
traditional use case

00:23:15.910 --> 00:23:17.860
where you might take a
photo of a whiteboard.

00:23:17.860 --> 00:23:19.330
But what this
allows you to do now

00:23:19.330 --> 00:23:22.420
is to take a digital copy of
the work you've been doing.

00:23:22.420 --> 00:23:24.550
So you just need to add
in the email address

00:23:24.550 --> 00:23:27.070
of your collaborator, and
this will allow you either

00:23:27.070 --> 00:23:29.680
to send a single
frame as a PNG or all

00:23:29.680 --> 00:23:32.290
of the frames in
your Jam as a PDF.

00:23:32.290 --> 00:23:34.540
We've seen a lot of
early-adopter customers

00:23:34.540 --> 00:23:36.260
use that in a number of ways.

00:23:36.260 --> 00:23:38.050
One of the most
frequent use cases

00:23:38.050 --> 00:23:42.280
is actually adding those
PDFs or PNGs to slide decks

00:23:42.280 --> 00:23:44.320
as well so they have a
ready-made image just

00:23:44.320 --> 00:23:46.464
to throw up on their slide.

00:23:46.464 --> 00:23:48.130
In addition to that
though, you actually

00:23:48.130 --> 00:23:51.020
might want to share this out and
have others collaborate on it

00:23:51.020 --> 00:23:51.520
with you.

00:23:51.520 --> 00:23:53.890
So in terms of doing
that, I talked a little

00:23:53.890 --> 00:23:57.150
before about our companion
app for phone and for tablet.

00:23:57.150 --> 00:23:59.750
To add collaborators,
you have two options.

00:23:59.750 --> 00:24:02.170
The first is, you just
hit share and just like

00:24:02.170 --> 00:24:05.530
we did with send the copy, you
add in their email address.

00:24:05.530 --> 00:24:07.330
The alternative to
doing that is actually

00:24:07.330 --> 00:24:08.980
using our broadcast code tool.

00:24:08.980 --> 00:24:10.540
So you can see at
the bottom here

00:24:10.540 --> 00:24:12.610
you have the broadcast
code for this Jam

00:24:12.610 --> 00:24:15.880
that we're currently on and the
option to join a new broadcast.

00:24:15.880 --> 00:24:18.850
That generates a six-digit
alphanumeric code

00:24:18.850 --> 00:24:21.970
that you can either enter
into a collaborative device,

00:24:21.970 --> 00:24:24.790
like a phone or a tablet,
or onto another Jamboard

00:24:24.790 --> 00:24:27.904
to collaborate with others.

00:24:27.904 --> 00:24:29.320
Did you want to
talk a little more

00:24:29.320 --> 00:24:32.020
about that kind of seamless,
walk-in, walk-out experience?

00:24:32.020 --> 00:24:32.800
TJ VARGHESE: Yeah.

00:24:32.800 --> 00:24:36.850
So we've also allowed
for your mobile apps

00:24:36.850 --> 00:24:43.190
to replace the need for you to
take a photo of a whiteboard.

00:24:43.190 --> 00:24:45.410
So you can actually
pull out your phone

00:24:45.410 --> 00:24:47.600
and you can find a
board that's nearby.

00:24:47.600 --> 00:24:51.110
So if you are in a workplace and
you have two boards in a room,

00:24:51.110 --> 00:24:53.990
it will actually tell you that
there is a board near to you,

00:24:53.990 --> 00:24:56.540
and you can actually claim
content from your Jam app

00:24:56.540 --> 00:24:59.810
on your phone, without
touching the board,

00:24:59.810 --> 00:25:03.010
and it will send a
notification to the board.

00:25:03.010 --> 00:25:05.600
And anyone who is at
the board can say, OK,

00:25:05.600 --> 00:25:08.060
you agree to allow to
give that person a copy.

00:25:08.060 --> 00:25:10.100
So, Lucy, you want to
give us a demo of that?

00:25:10.100 --> 00:25:11.100
LUCY BOURNE: Sure thing.

00:25:11.100 --> 00:25:13.370
So I actually have a
Jam up here that one

00:25:13.370 --> 00:25:15.617
of our creative designers
was working on earlier.

00:25:15.617 --> 00:25:17.450
And I want to push it
up so you can see some

00:25:17.450 --> 00:25:18.616
of the cool stuff he's done.

00:25:18.616 --> 00:25:20.780
So I'm in the app on my
phone, and I'm actually

00:25:20.780 --> 00:25:23.240
in that active Jam that
I was talking about.

00:25:23.240 --> 00:25:26.020
In the top corner we have
a small icon for our board,

00:25:26.020 --> 00:25:28.010
and I'm going to do a
search and look for what

00:25:28.010 --> 00:25:29.720
boards are close by here.

00:25:29.720 --> 00:25:33.120
So I'm going to try
breakout board one.

00:25:33.120 --> 00:25:36.200
And what this does is it
allows me to push this Jam up

00:25:36.200 --> 00:25:38.590
onto the board.

00:25:38.590 --> 00:25:40.270
BARINE TEE: Wrong board, Lucy.

00:25:40.270 --> 00:25:41.120
That board.

00:25:41.120 --> 00:25:42.949
LUCY BOURNE: So, Barine, well--

00:25:42.949 --> 00:25:44.990
BARINE TEE: But as you
can see, Lucy is sort of--

00:25:44.990 --> 00:25:47.450
she's taken that same Jam
and she's pushed it on here.

00:25:47.450 --> 00:25:50.510
And within a few seconds,
that Jam content that she had

00:25:50.510 --> 00:25:51.732
shows up on the board.

00:25:51.732 --> 00:25:54.065
So imagine a scenario like
Lucy said-- she's walking out

00:25:54.065 --> 00:25:54.940
of a conference room.

00:25:54.940 --> 00:25:56.765
She got kicked out
because you know she--

00:25:56.765 --> 00:25:57.890
TJ VARGHESE: Ran over time.

00:25:57.890 --> 00:25:59.840
BARINE TEE: She goes somewhere
else to another Jamboard,

00:25:59.840 --> 00:26:01.840
and just like that she
can continue her session.

00:26:07.310 --> 00:26:08.039
TJ VARGHESE: OK.

00:26:08.039 --> 00:26:09.580
So for a lot of the
tech enthusiasts,

00:26:09.580 --> 00:26:12.270
you're probably wondering, what
does the Jamboard come with?

00:26:12.270 --> 00:26:13.800
I'm going to hand it back
to Barine to talk about--

00:26:13.800 --> 00:26:15.674
BARINE TEE: What's
actually in this Jamboard?

00:26:15.674 --> 00:26:17.146
What's powering this Jamboard?

00:26:17.146 --> 00:26:18.520
So I'll quickly
go over to specs.

00:26:18.520 --> 00:26:21.790
And I'll go somewhere I
can actually see the specs.

00:26:21.790 --> 00:26:26.440
Well to start, the main idea,
just like TJ said earlier,

00:26:26.440 --> 00:26:28.210
we want the technology
to disappear.

00:26:28.210 --> 00:26:30.170
We want the latencies to
be as low as possible.

00:26:30.170 --> 00:26:32.628
This way, it doesn't-- you're
not fighting against yourself

00:26:32.628 --> 00:26:34.024
to actually use this device.

00:26:34.024 --> 00:26:35.440
But I'll just read
this down here.

00:26:35.440 --> 00:26:40.660
It's a 55 inch, 4K, UHD display,
120 Hertz touch scan rate,

00:26:40.660 --> 00:26:43.189
60 Hertz video refresh rate.

00:26:43.189 --> 00:26:44.980
We've got the wide
angle camera on the top.

00:26:44.980 --> 00:26:47.313
This is so you can actually
do a video-to-video call in,

00:26:47.313 --> 00:26:50.800
as well as taking
images and adding

00:26:50.800 --> 00:26:52.540
the content to the Jamboard.

00:26:52.540 --> 00:26:54.470
Down-firing speakers as well.

00:26:54.470 --> 00:26:56.260
There's an NFC module built in.

00:26:56.260 --> 00:26:58.960
There's also Type-C built
in, so I can actually

00:26:58.960 --> 00:27:00.940
charge my laptop,
charge my phone.

00:27:00.940 --> 00:27:03.100
I can actually connect
my laptop in there

00:27:03.100 --> 00:27:05.840
and use the Jamboard
as a video screen.

00:27:05.840 --> 00:27:09.770
It accepts video-in as well
through the Type-C. HDMI 2.0,

00:27:09.770 --> 00:27:11.370
it's the 1.4 spec.

00:27:11.370 --> 00:27:12.820
USB 3.0 as well, too.

00:27:12.820 --> 00:27:14.830
And obviously, one of
the staple features

00:27:14.830 --> 00:27:16.750
here is the passive stylus.

00:27:16.750 --> 00:27:19.180
Passive stylus, passive eraser.

00:27:19.180 --> 00:27:22.900
And the Jamboard will know the
difference between the eraser

00:27:22.900 --> 00:27:24.520
and the stylus,
regardless of the fact

00:27:24.520 --> 00:27:26.359
that nothing's in here.

00:27:26.359 --> 00:27:28.150
LUCY BOURNE: And one
thing I wanted to add,

00:27:28.150 --> 00:27:29.980
which we haven't
been able to display

00:27:29.980 --> 00:27:31.830
during the last few
days, but the board

00:27:31.830 --> 00:27:34.360
can be wall-mounted as
well as stand-mounted.

00:27:34.360 --> 00:27:36.670
So in terms of the
use case for a stand,

00:27:36.670 --> 00:27:40.120
we've seen it's hugely popular
for things like team breakout

00:27:40.120 --> 00:27:42.950
rooms or for collaboration
across different teams

00:27:42.950 --> 00:27:44.890
so they can move
it between spaces.

00:27:44.890 --> 00:27:46.750
The wall mount is a
really terrific option

00:27:46.750 --> 00:27:48.670
for a permanently placed board.

00:27:48.670 --> 00:27:51.010
And we've seen teams
do that a lot more with

00:27:51.010 --> 00:27:54.190
four-to-six-person meeting
rooms where there's a lot

00:27:54.190 --> 00:27:56.005
of collaboration happening.

00:27:56.005 --> 00:27:57.380
TJ VARGHESE: And
one last feature

00:27:57.380 --> 00:27:59.030
that we didn't put it here on
the slides, because we didn't

00:27:59.030 --> 00:28:00.500
necessarily want
you to try it out,

00:28:00.500 --> 00:28:03.360
is it has Google
Cast built into it.

00:28:03.360 --> 00:28:06.080
So if you ever have
slides or other content

00:28:06.080 --> 00:28:08.390
that you want to put here
and use this as a display,

00:28:08.390 --> 00:28:11.760
you can with Google Cast.

00:28:11.760 --> 00:28:15.570
So, like I said at the start,
we wanted to price it right.

00:28:15.570 --> 00:28:18.840
It's going to be available
for purchase in May.

00:28:18.840 --> 00:28:22.170
It will be priced at $4,999.

00:28:22.170 --> 00:28:25.260
There's a $600 annual
management support fee,

00:28:25.260 --> 00:28:29.670
and we are going to be running
a initial promotion at 50% off.

00:28:33.230 --> 00:28:37.290
And all of this is going
to get so much better.

00:28:40.990 --> 00:28:46.800
Sorry, to clarify, it's 50% of
the annual subscription fee.

00:28:46.800 --> 00:28:48.930
Everybody saw that
slide very well.

00:28:48.930 --> 00:28:51.000
BARINE TEE: Just for you guys.

00:28:51.000 --> 00:28:53.150
[MUSIC]

